CAT = "phcleCorrections"; SEG = "COR"
# Correctional Administration: institutional correction (BuCor, BJMP, provincial jails,
# R.A. 10592), non-institutional correction (P.D. 968 as amended by R.A. 10707, Act 4103,
# executive clemency) and therapeutic modalities.
Q = [
("The Bureau of Corrections, which keeps national prisoners, is under the:",
 "Department of Justice", ["Department of the Interior and Local Government", "Department of Social Welfare and Development", "Supreme Court"],
 "The Bureau of Corrections is a DOJ agency (R.A. 10575, the Bureau of Corrections Act of 2013). The BJMP, which runs city and municipal jails, is under the DILG."),
("A prisoner sentenced to a term of more than three years is classed as a:",
 "National prisoner", ["Provincial prisoner", "City prisoner", "Municipal prisoner"],
 "Prisoners with sentences of more than three years serve them in national penitentiaries under the Bureau of Corrections. Shorter terms are served in provincial, city or municipal jails."),
("District, city and municipal jails are under the supervision and control of the:",
 "Bureau of Jail Management and Penology", ["Bureau of Corrections", "Philippine National Police", "Department of Social Welfare and Development"],
 "R.A. 6975 gave the BJMP control of district, city and municipal jails. Provincial jails remain under the provincial governments."),
("The written order of a court directing a jail to receive and hold a convicted or accused person is the:",
 "Mittimus", ["Subpoena", "Habeas corpus", "Warrant of arrest"],
 "A mittimus commits a person to jail and is the jail's authority to hold him. A writ of habeas corpus orders the custodian to produce the detainee and justify the detention."),
("Under R.A. 10592, a prisoner of good conduct in his first two years of imprisonment earns a deduction of:",
 "20 days for each month", ["15 days for each month", "23 days for each month", "30 days for each month"],
 "R.A. 10592 grants 20 days a month in years one and two, 23 days in years three to five, 25 days in years six to ten, and 30 days from the eleventh year on."),
("Under R.A. 10592, a prisoner who studies, teaches or mentors while serving his sentence may earn an additional deduction of:",
 "15 days for each month", ["10 days for each month", "20 days for each month", "5 days for each month"],
 "The time allowance for study, teaching or mentoring (TASTM) is 15 days for each month, on top of the good conduct time allowance."),
("A prisoner who escapes during a disorder or calamity and gives himself up within 48 hours after it ends earns a deduction of:",
 "One-fifth of his sentence", ["One-half of his sentence", "One-third of his sentence", "Two-fifths of his sentence"],
 "Article 98 of the Revised Penal Code, as amended by R.A. 10592, grants one-fifth for those who return within 48 hours, and two-fifths for those who chose to stay in prison during the calamity."),
("Under P.D. 968, the Probation Law, probation is granted by:",
 "The court that convicted him", ["The Board of Pardons and Parole", "The President", "The Bureau of Corrections"],
 "An application is filed with the trial court that convicted the defendant, and that court decides after a post-sentence investigation by a probation officer. Parole is granted by the Board of Pardons and Parole."),
("Under P.D. 968 as amended, probation is not available to an offender sentenced to a maximum term of imprisonment of:",
 "More than 6 years", ["More than 1 year", "More than 3 years", "More than 12 years"],
 "Section 9 disqualifies offenders sentenced to more than six years of imprisonment, along with those convicted of crimes against national security and certain others."),
("Under R.A. 10707, an accused who appeals a conviction for a non-probationable penalty, and on appeal gets a probationable penalty, may:",
 "Still apply for probation", ["No longer apply for probation", "Apply only for parole", "Apply only after serving half the sentence"],
 "R.A. 10707 amended P.D. 968 so that when the appellate court reduces the penalty to a probationable one, the accused may apply for probation upon remand to the trial court."),
("Under the Indeterminate Sentence Law (Act 4103), a prisoner may be released on parole after serving:",
 "The minimum of his sentence", ["The maximum of his sentence", "One-half of his maximum sentence", "Ten years regardless of sentence"],
 "The court fixes a minimum and a maximum term. After serving the minimum, the prisoner may be paroled by the Board of Pardons and Parole."),
("Under the 1987 Constitution, the President may grant amnesty only with the concurrence of:",
 "A majority of all the members of Congress", ["Two-thirds of all the members of the Senate", "The Supreme Court", "The Department of Justice"],
 "Article VII, Section 19 allows the President to grant amnesty with the concurrence of a majority of all the members of Congress. Pardon, reprieve and commutation need no such concurrence."),
("A form of executive clemency that reduces a sentence to a lighter one is:",
 "Commutation", ["Reprieve", "Amnesty", "Absolute pardon"],
 "Commutation changes the penalty to a lesser one. A reprieve postpones the execution of a sentence, and amnesty is a public act that covers classes of persons, usually for political offenses."),
("The prison reformer who wrote \"The State of the Prisons\" in 1777 was:",
 "John Howard", ["Walter Crofton", "Zebulon Brockway", "Alexander Maconochie"],
 "John Howard, sheriff of Bedfordshire, reported on the conditions of English and European prisons. Crofton created the Irish system and Brockway ran the Elmira Reformatory."),
("The prison system that kept prisoners in solitary confinement day and night, with work in their cells, was the:",
 "Pennsylvania system", ["Auburn system", "Irish system", "Elmira system"],
 "The Pennsylvania (separate) system isolated prisoners completely. The Auburn (congregate) system let them work together by day in silence and sleep in separate cells."),
("Alexander Maconochie, who let prisoners earn early release through marks for work and good behavior, is called the father of:",
 "Parole", ["Probation", "Solitary confinement", "The reformatory"],
 "Maconochie's mark system on Norfolk Island let prisoners earn their way out, the basis of parole. John Augustus of Boston is called the father of probation."),
("The penal colony in Palawan, established in 1904, where prisoners live and farm in open settlements, is the:",
 "Iwahig Prison and Penal Farm", ["Davao Prison and Penal Farm", "San Ramon Prison and Penal Farm", "Sablayan Prison and Penal Farm"],
 "Iwahig in Puerto Princesa City is an open penal colony. San Ramon is in Zamboanga, Davao in Davao del Norte and Sablayan in Occidental Mindoro."),
("The treatment model in which drug-dependent inmates live as a family, using peer pressure and self-help to change behavior, is the:",
 "Therapeutic Community", ["Work release program", "Halfway house", "Mark system"],
 "The Therapeutic Community modality treats the community itself as the agent of change, with structured roles, group sessions and rules. It is used by the BuCor and the BJMP."),
("Inmates classified as highly dangerous or escape risks, who need the highest degree of control, are:",
 "Maximum security prisoners", ["Medium security prisoners", "Minimum security prisoners", "Detainees for trial"],
 "Maximum security inmates are kept under the strictest supervision. Minimum security inmates can be trusted with less supervision, for example in penal farm work."),
("Time spent in detention while a case is pending, when the detainee agreed to abide by the same rules as convicted prisoners, is:",
 "Credited in full to the sentence", ["Not credited at all", "Credited at half its length", "Credited only for crimes against property"],
 "Article 29 of the Revised Penal Code, as amended by R.A. 10592, credits preventive imprisonment in full if the detainee agreed in writing to the same disciplinary rules as convicts, and at four-fifths otherwise."),
]
